No. **“The IRS does not care about the form” is unsupported.** The missed 30-day election remains legally consequential.

If defensible evidence shows that each tranche’s fair market value at vesting was no greater than its allocated purchase price, the default §83(a) calculation may produce **$0 compensation income**. Later appreciation may then be capital gain. That is compliance with the default rules—not substantial compliance, tolerance of an omission, or an audit-risk bet.

You would still need to determine, tranche by tranche:

- fair market value and spread at vesting;
- basis;
- capital-gain holding period, generally beginning at vesting;
- potential §1202/QSBS timing;
- any payroll or information-reporting obligations.

The accurate position is: **no valid §83(b) election; calculate tax under §83(a), using documented vesting-date values.** Do not base the return position on presumed IRS indifference.
